This is a partial sample because the PET tool is used here which does code mutation and generation for .NET. In the given code, some code lines are specific to PET and are removed. But the basic functionality and overall structure remains. Replace //Code related to arraylist add operation with the actual logic of the add method and replace //Add operation related assertion with the appropriate assertion statements.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount - 1);
</code>

This assertion is because after adding an element, the count should increase by one. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_ret != Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion is because after removing an element, the count should decrease by one (unless the removed element was not in the array).

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_index);
</code>

This assertion is because after inserting an element, the index should not decrease. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_index);
</code>

This assertion is because after setting an element, the index should not change. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(arrList[index] == Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion is because if there is no such index, an exception will be thrown which can be caught in a try-catch statement. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_Ret == Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion is because after inserting or setting an element, the IndexOf the element should not decrease, unless it was not in the array, in which case it should increase by one.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
</code>

This assertion is because after adding, inserting, or setting an element, the LastIndexOf the element should not decrease or increase, unless it was not in the array, in which case it should increase by one.

<code>
Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX && !New_Ret) || (!Old_arrListContainsX && New_Ret));
</code>

This assertion is because after removing an element, it should no longer be contained in the array.

<code>
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion is because the Count property does not change.

<code>
Debug.Assert((Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX) || (Old_arrListIndexOfX > New_arrListIndexOfX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX > New_arrListLastIndexOfX) || New_arrListIndexOfX == -1, "New array list contains the same element(s) as old array list, and that element(s) are not in the same order");
</code>

This assertion is because if elements added to array list have the same order as old array list, their indices in the new array list ought to remain same; if not, their indices should point to the next empty place. Also, if the new array list does not contain elements from the old array list, index of old elements in new array should be -1.--------------------